%description:

RFC 9000 -- flow control.

Checks RFC9000-FC-1 (must), from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc9000/catalog.md. The English
procedure and mockup are in doc/project/evidence/protocol/quic/checks/flow-control.md#flow-control; the program
steps below map one to one onto its expected observations. Mockup mapping: host A = client, host B
= server.

Scenario: host B's (the server's) initial stream flow-control limit is set to 2000 octets
(`**.server.quic.initialMaxStreamData = 2000B` -- the parameter is declared `int
initialMaxStreamData @unit(B)` in src/inet/transportlayer/quic/Quic.ned, so a bare byte literal is
the right unit), smaller than the 4000-octet block, so the limit binds (checks.md's own scenario
constants for this check). `sendMaxDataFramesImmediately` and `roundConsumedDataValue` are also set
on host B, mirroring examples/quic/flow_control_limited/omnetpp.ini's own working configuration of
the same mechanism, so the receiver raises the limit as soon as it has consumed enough rather than
waiting to piggyback the MAX_STREAM_DATA frame on some other outgoing packet. Host A otherwise uses
the same 4000-octet, one-shot, non-closing TrafficgenCompound+QuicTrafficgen scenario as
Rfc9000ConnectionEstablishment.test.

Implementation of observation 2 ("the total stream data host A sends, before any frame from host B
raises the limit, does not exceed 2000 octets"): the task brief suggested either a `never` step for
an over-limit STREAM frame before the first MAX_STREAM_DATA, or a sum accumulated in a lambda.
Neither is directly usable as first imagined, for reasons specific to this engine (confirmed by
reading tests/protocol/lib/ProtocolTester.cc/EventPattern.cc, not just guessed):
  - A running byte sum can't be accumulated on the *signal* side: EventPattern::selectorMatches()
    hard-blocks `.match()`/`.packet()` on a scalar (non-packet) event -- see the deviation note in
    Rfc9000StreamDataTransfer.test -- so this would have to run on packet events, which is exactly
    what this test does (see below).
  - A `never` step naturally expresses "must not happen", but a `never` (like any greedy
    Count-backed step) only resolves -- and hence only lets the *next* step start watching the
    event stream -- once its own `.within()` window fully elapses (confirmed in
    ProtocolTester.cc's handleMessage(), StepType::Never/Count deadline handling). This engine is
    strictly sequential and single-pass (exactly one step is "current" at a time; a non-matching
    event is simply gone once a step has looked at it -- confirmed in ProtocolTester::processMatch()
    switching on `program->steps[currentStep]`). The gap between "host A's initial burst finishes"
    and "host B's first MAX_STREAM_DATA arrives" is under 200 microseconds in this scenario (checked
    empirically with a throwaway probe), far too small a target to size a `never` step's `.within()`
    against reliably -- and any value large enough to be robust would run past the point where host
    A finishes sending the remainder, so a subsequent step looking for that completion would never
    see it (its event already went by while the long `never` was still "current").
  - A predicate cannot signal failure by throwing, either: EventPattern::selectorMatches() wraps
    every `.match()` call in a try/catch that turns *any* exception into a silent non-match (by
    design, so a predicate that peeks a field absent from an unrelated packet fails safe) -- so a
    `throw` from inside a lambda can never surface as a test failure here.
  - What *does* survive across steps cleanly: `once` steps advance the instant they match, so they
    never withhold events from whatever step follows -- and a `once` step's predicate, even while
    returning false and "still waiting", still runs once per candidate event, so it can perform side
    effects. This test therefore threads two `std::shared_ptr<bool>` flags (`raised`, `violated`)
    through three sequential `once` steps' predicates. Every predicate call first records, as a side
    effect, whether the event is host B raising the limit or host A sending stream data that (before
    any raise) exceeds it; only *then* does it evaluate its own step's specific match condition,
    which for the "raise" step also requires `!*violated`. Since events are examined by whichever
    step is current with no gaps between steps, this reconstructs a `never`-like invariant over the
    causal interval "before the first raise" exactly, without depending on any guessed timing: if a
    violation is ever recorded, the "raise" step can never match (its own condition includes
    `!*violated`, permanently false from then on) and fails honestly by deadline miss, naming the
    step and its `.describe()` text -- a real, visible, correctly-attributed failure, not a silently
    swallowed one.

Implementation note: as in the other QUIC tests in this suite, evalPacketField()/.packet("quic....")
cannot see inside a QUIC packet; this test reads the STREAM and MAX_STREAM_DATA frames' fields
directly off the packet's own chunk tree via QuicChunks.h's findQuicChunk<T>().

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "QuicChunks.h"
#include <memory>

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    // Shared, mutable, causal-state flags threaded through the three steps below -- see the
    // %description's implementation note for why this replaces a timing-based never/window.
    auto raised = std::make_shared<bool>(false);     // host B has sent a MAX_STREAM_DATA frame
    auto violated = std::make_shared<bool>(false);   // a STREAM frame exceeded 2000 before that

    // Side effect only, run from every step's predicate on every candidate event: keeps
    // `raised`/`violated` correct regardless of which step is "current" when the event arrives.
    auto track = [raised, violated](const PacketEvent& e) {
        if (e.packet == nullptr)
            return;
        if (e.signalName == "packetSentToLower") {
            if (auto sf = findQuicChunk<StreamFrameHeader>(e.packet)) {
                if (!*raised && sf->getOffset() + sf->getLength() > 2000)
                    *violated = true;
            }
        }
        else if (e.signalName == "packetReceivedFromLower") {
            if (findQuicChunk<MaxStreamDataFrameHeader>(e.packet))
                *raised = true;
        }
    };

    return ProtocolTest("rfc9000_flow_control")
        // Expected observation 1: host A sends a datagram whose QUIC packet carries a
        // STREAM frame. Confirms the stimulus.
        .once(on("client.udp")
                  .match([track](const MatchContext& c) {
                      track(c.event);
                      return c.event.signalName == "packetSentToLower"
                          && findQuicChunk<StreamFrameHeader>(c.event.packet) != nullptr;
                  })
                  .describe("host A's first STREAM frame")
                  .within(0.5))
        // Expected observation 2 (the whole of RFC9000-FC-1: the limit binds a sender, it
        // does not merely inform it) folded into the match condition for observation 3's
        // first half: this step can only match a MAX_STREAM_DATA frame for which no
        // earlier STREAM frame (tracked continuously since the previous step, see above)
        // has already exceeded 2000 octets. If one had, `*violated` stays true forever and
        // this step fails by deadline miss instead -- see the %description.
        .once(on("client.udp")
                  .match([track, violated](const MatchContext& c) {
                      track(c.event);
                      return !*violated
                          && c.event.signalName == "packetReceivedFromLower"
                          && findQuicChunk<MaxStreamDataFrameHeader>(c.event.packet) != nullptr;
                  })
                  .describe("host B raises the stream flow-control limit, with no STREAM frame from host A having exceeded 2000 octets beforehand")
                  .within(1.0))
        // Expected observation 3, second half: host A then sends the remainder, so that
        // host B's application receives all 4000 octets -- observed, as in
        // Rfc9000StreamDataTransfer.test, as the STREAM frame whose offset+length
        // completes the 4000-octet block (no loss/reordering in this mockup, so this is
        // the last one sent).
        .once(on("client.udp")
                  .match([track](const MatchContext& c) {
                      track(c.event);
                      if (c.event.signalName != "packetSentToLower")
                          return false;
                      auto sf = findQuicChunk<StreamFrameHeader>(c.event.packet);
                      return sf != nullptr && sf->getOffset() + sf->getLength() == 4000;
                  })
                  .describe("host A sends the remainder, completing all 4000 octets")
                  .within(2.0));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc9000_flow_control compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// Mockup of doc/project/evidence/protocol/quic/checks.md's "Common mockup":
// host A (client) -- path P -- host B (server).
network QuicNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        client: StandardHost { @display("p=250,100"); }
        server: StandardHost { @display("p=450,100"); }
    connections:
        client.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> server.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = QuicNet
sim-time-limit = 5s
seed-set = 0

**.quic.typename = "Quic"

# client: a single 4000-octet burst on one stream (checks.md's common-mockup data block).
**.client.hasQuic = true
**.client.numApps = 1
**.client.app[0].typename = "TrafficgenCompound"
**.client.app[0].numGenerators = 1
**.client.app[0].generator[0].typename = "TrafficgenSimple"
**.client.app[0].generator[0].id = 0
**.client.app[0].generator[0].packetSize = 4000B
**.client.app[0].generator[0].packetCount = -1
**.client.app[0].generator[0].packetInterval = 10s
**.client.app[0].generator[0].startTime = 0.1s
**.client.app[0].generator[0].ordered = false
**.client.app[0].handler.typename = "QuicTrafficgen"
**.client.app[0].handler.localPort = 1001
**.client.app[0].handler.connectPort = 1000
**.client.app[0].handler.connectAddress = "server"

# server: reads and discards everything it receives, with its initial stream flow-control
# limit set below the 4000-octet block so the limit binds (checks.md's scenario constants).
**.server.hasQuic = true
**.server.numApps = 1
**.server.app[*].typename = "QuicDiscardServer"
**.server.app[*].localAddress = ""
**.server.app[*].localPort = 1000
**.server.quic.initialMaxStreamData = 2000B
**.server.quic.sendMaxDataFramesImmediately = true
**.server.quic.roundConsumedDataValue = true

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc9000_flow_control: PASS
